ns.\n\nBest move is to **discard a loose terminal Character tile** that doesn\u2019t contribute to any strong shape. Among W1, W2, W4, W7 \u2014 W1 and W2 could theoretically form a chow with W3, but W3 is already melded, so those are now useless for that purpose. W4 and W7 are spaced apart.\n\nW7 is far from other W tiles and least likely to form a chow now. Similarly, W1 and W2 are low-end, but since W3 is gone, they cannot form chows unless someone discards W2 or W1 respectively.\n\nBut among these, **W7** is the most isolated \u2014 no adjacent tiles in hand, and no sign of building upper-end chows. Discarding **W7** minimizes damage and removes a stranded tile.\n\nHowever, consider safety: F4 is my wind pair. B3 is a pair. Keeping both pairs might help later. But holding too many near-wins can slow progress.\n\nActually, **W4** is more dangerous to keep \u2014 it could be part of middle sequences, but I have no W5/W6. Still, W4-W5-W6 chow is possible, and W5/W6 are still available. W7 has fewer combinations (needs W5/W6 or W8/W9 \u2014 but W8/W9 are common, though not in my hand).\n\nWait: I already have a W3 pung. If I keep W1, W2, W4, they are all disconnected from that.\n\nBut **W1 and W2 together** could imply a lower-chance [W1,W2,W3] was possible before the Peng \u2014 but now it\u2019s irrelevant.\n\nGiven that, **all W tiles except one should go**. Best to keep **W1 and W2** because they can form a chow if I draw W3 \u2014 but W3 is already fully used (I have a pung, and only one unshown W3 left), so drawing another is impossible. There are only 4 copies. I used 3 in Peng, and 1 remains unshown \u2014 possibly in wall or someone\u2019s hand. So W3 is nearly exhausted.\n\nThus, **W1, W2, W4, W7 are all dead ends**.\n\nSo the decision is which to discard.\n\nBut note: **F4\u00d72** is valuable due to Seat and Prevalent Wind (both South = F2? Wait: correction needed!)\n\nWait: Prevailing Wind is **South Wind**, which is **F2**, not F4.\n\nAnd your Seat Wind is **South Wind**, so also **F2**.\n\nYou said: \"The Prevailing Wind is: South Wind. Your Seat Wind is: South Wind.\"\n\nSo **F2 is the relevant wind**, not F4.\n\nF4 is North Wind.\n\nSo your **F4 pair is NOT Seat or Prevalent Wind**.\n\nThat changes everything.\n\nYou have **F4\u00d72 (North Wind)**, which scores nothing for wind patterns unless you make a pung.\n\nBut **F2** is the important wind \u2014 and it's already been Peng'd by the previous player (they have [F2,F2,F2]), so all four F2s are accounted for (3 in their meld, 1 discarded earlier). So F2 is gone.\n\nTherefore, **you cannot score Prevalent or Seat Wind** \u2014 it's taken.\n\nSo **F4 pair is just a regular honor pair**, worth only \u201cPung of Honors\u201d if you get a third, or \u201cPair\u201d in winning hand.\n\nSimilarly, your **B3\u00d72** is a better candidate for a pair \u2014 it's a simple tile, but usable.\n\nNow, re-evaluate:\n\n- You need a winning pair. Candidates: B3\u00d72 or F4\u00d72.\n- B3 is better because it's part of the same suit as your existing chow [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 wait, **you already used B3 in the chow**?\n\nYour meld is [B3, B4, B5] \u2014 so **one B3 is already used in the chow**.\n\nYou have **two B3s in hand**, plus **one B3 in the chow** \u2192 total **three B3s used**.\n\nThere are only 4 copies of each tile.\n\nSo only **one B3 remains unshown**.\n\nYou have two B3s in hand, and one in the meld \u2192 yes, three total.\n\nSo you cannot have a fourth unless it\u2019s drawn.\n\nBut you **cannot use B3 in two places** \u2014 either it's in the chow or in the pair.\n\nYour hand tiles: [W1, W2, W4, W7, B3, B3, F4, F4]\n\nMelds: [W3,W3,W3], [B3,B4,B5]\n\nIn the chow [B3,B4,B5], one B3 is used.\n\nSo total B3s: one in meld, two in hand \u2192 **three B3s**.\n\nYou cannot form a pair of B3 unless you discard one \u2014 otherwise you have three separate B3s, which isn't a pair.\n\nAh! This is critical.\n\nCurrently, you have **three B3 tiles**: one in the chow, two in hand.\n\nBut in terms of hand composition, the **chow uses one B3**, and your **hand holds two B3s**, so when counting final hand, you have:\n\n- One B3 in exposed chow\n- Two B3s in hand \u2192 this forms a **pair of B3**\n\nYes: total of three B3s \u2014 one used in meld, two unused forming a pair. That\u2019s fine.\n\nFor example, you can have a hand with a chow using B3, and a pair of B3 \u2014 that\u2019s allowed, as long as you don\u2019t exceed four copies.\n\nSo your **pair is either B3\u00d72 or F4\u00d72**.\n\nBoth are possible.\n\nBut **B3 is a terminal** (number 1), so pairing B3 gives a terminal pair, while F4 is a wind.\n\nNo advantage.\n\nBut consider **scoring patterns**.\n\nCan you go for **Outside Hand** (8 points)? It requires every meld and the pair to contain at least one terminal or honor.\n\nCheck current melds:\n- [W3,W3,W3]: W3 is a middle tile (not terminal), and not honor \u2192 **does NOT qualify**\n- [B3,B4,B5]: contains B3 (terminal), so qualifies\n\nSo the W3 pung breaks Outside Hand.\n\nTherefore, **Outside Hand is impossible**.\n\nSimilarly, **All Simples** is broken by W3 pung and honor tiles.\n\nSo no clean pattern.\n\nBack to discards.\n\nSince W3 pung is already formed and breaks Outside Hand and All Simples, you\u2019re likely heading toward a **Chicken Hand** (8 points) \u2014 minimal scoring.\n\nTo reach 8 points, you need at least:\n- Chicken Hand (8 points) \u2014 but only if no other pattern applies.\n\nBut you might have:\n- All Pungs? No \u2014 you have one pung (W3), one chow ([B3,B4,B5]) \u2014 so not all pungs.\n- To get All Pungs, you\u2019d need to convert or replace the chow.\n\nImpossible now.\n\n- Half Flush? You have W and B suited, plus F4 honors \u2014 not pure.\n\n- One Voided Suit? You have W and B, missing T? But you had T tiles in history, but none now \u2014 but T is a suit you\u2019ve used, so you have all three suits involved? Wait: you had T1,T2,T3,T5,T6,T7,T8,T9 played \u2014 so you touched Bamboo suit.\n\nBut current hand: only W and B and F4 \u2014 so **only two suits**: Characters and Dots, plus honors.\n\nSo **One Voided Suit** (1 point) is possible if you win.\n\nAlso, **No Honors** is broken.\n\nBut you have honor tiles.\n\nNow, best strategy: **minimize loss, aim for Chicken Hand**.\n\nYou need one more meld and a secure pair.\n\nCurrent: two melds, five single tiles.\n\nYou need to form two more melds or one more meld and improve the pair.\n\nBut you must discard one now.\n\nTop priority: **keep flexibility**.\n\nDiscard a tile that is least likely to help.\n\nAmong the options:\n- **W1, W2, W4, W7, B3, F4**\n\nB3: you have two, forming a pair. Discarding one weakens your pair \u2014 risky.\nF4: same \u2014 pair of winds. Also risky.\nW1, W2, W4, W7: all single, no support.\n\nBut **W7** is highest and most isolated. No W6, W8, W9 in hand. W8 and W9 are still available, but you have no draws.\n\nSimilarly, W1 and W2 could start a [W1,W2,W3] but W3 is gone.\n\nBut **W4** is slightly better \u2014 could form [W4,W5,W6] if you draw W5/W6.\n\nW5 and W6 are relatively available (4 each unshown).\n\nWhereas W7 needs W6/W8 or W8/W9 \u2014 W8 has only 2 left, W9 has 4.\n\nStill, low chance.\n\nBut **W1 and W2 together** could be kept to potentially form a chow if W3 becomes available \u2014 but W3 only one left, and likely in wall or opponent.\n\nBut **Chi is only from previous player**, who just played T5 \u2014 not a W tile.\n\nSo no immediate Chi chance.\n\nThus, all W tiles are weak.\n\nBut **discard W7**: it\u2019s the farthest from others, and W7-W8-W9 is upper end, but you have no affinity.\n\nMoreover, **T7,T8,T9** is already a meld of the across player \u2014 they have [T7,T8,T9], so they may be collecting high Bamboos.\n\nBut not directly related.\n\nAlso, **B8 is played by multiple players** \u2014 B8 only 1 unshown, so rare.\n\nBut not helpful.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nKeep **F4\u00d72** for possible wind pair (honors).\nKeep **B3\u00d72** for terminal pair.\nKeep **W1,W2,W4** for possible chows.\nBut must discard one.\n\nBest discard: **W7**, because:\n- It\u2019s isolated\n- Doesn\u2019t connect to any existing sequence\n- High number, less flexible\n- Other W tiles (W1,W2,W4) have slightly better potential for middle or lower chows\n\nAlternatively, **W4** is in the middle, might be more useful for [W4,W5,W6].\n\nW1 and W2 can form [W1,W2,W3] \u2014 but W3 is nearly gone.\n\nBut still, W7 is worse.\n\nAlso, **no one has shown interest in low Characters**, so W1/W2 might be safer discards later.\n\nBut for now, **W7 is the weakest tile**.\n\nAnswer: Play W7\n\nWait \u2014 valid actions include **Play W7**, yes.\n\nBut let\u2019s confirm: do you have W7? Hand: [W1, W2, W4, W7, B3\u00d72, F4\u00d72] \u2014 yes.\n\nSo discard W7.\n\nReason: W7 is an isolated tile with no connection to existing melds or potential chows, while other tiles like W1, W2, W4 have marginal potential to form lower/middle chows in the Character suit. Retaining B3\u00d72 and F4\u00d72 preserves two possible winning pairs. The hand is likely aiming for a Chicken Hand (8 points), and removing the least useful tile maximizes future flexibility.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
